<h1>Video 094 — Green Thread, stop(), suspend(), resume()</h1>
<h2>Video info</h2>
<p>Title: Core Java with OCJP/SCJP: Multi Threading Part-14 || Green Thread,stop(),suspend(),resume()</p>
<p>Source: real YouTube auto-captions (.transcripts/094-21KT--VtawI.txt). ASR decoded in place: green thread = Green Thread; native o / native W / native Ys / underlying Bo / underlying voice = native OS / underlying OS; JM / jbm = JVM; Sun Solaris = Sun Solaris; stop / suppress = stop() (deprecated); suspend / resume = setSuspend/resume → suspend() / resume(); weight = wait; Ria / runable = Runnable/ready; dat / de / that state = dead state; ENT / interent = interview; keep subing / keep supping / keep keeping = keep writing.</p>
<p>This short Part-14 covers Green Thread model vs Native OS model, why stop() / suspend() / resume() are deprecated, a small stop() demo with deprecation warning, then the full thread life-cycle diagram tying yield, join, sleep, wait/notify, suspend/resume, and stop together for interviews.</p>
<h3>00:07 — Topic: Green Thread (interview)</h3>
<p>Joke: do not expect “Red Thread / Yellow Thread” next — only one concept named Green Thread.</p>
<p>Do not say in interview: “thread which is in green color.”</p>
<h3>00:55 — Two models of Java multi-threading</h3>
<p>Java multi-threading is implemented using two models:</p>
<ol><li>Green Thread model</li></ol>
<ol><li>Native OS model</li></ol>
<h3>01:29 — Green Thread model (definition)</h3>
<p>Green thread: thread managed completely by the JVM without taking underlying OS support.</p>
<p>That model = Green Thread model.</p>
<p>Drawback: without OS/processor-level support, some features may not work properly. OS support is often required.</p>
<p>Support: Most operating systems won’t provide support for green thread model. Very few OSes like Sun Solaris provided support.</p>
<p>Status: Green thread model is deprecated and not recommended to use. We are not using it currently.</p>
<h3>02:56 — Native OS model</h3>
<p>Native OS model: thread life cycle managed by the JVM with the help of underlying OS.</p>
<p>All Windows-based operating systems provide support for Native OS model only.</p>
<p>Interview answer:</p>
<ul><li>Green thread = completely managed by JVM without underlying OS support.</li></ul>
<ul><li>With underlying OS support → native OS model / native threads.</li></ul>
<pre class='code'> // Board:
 // Java multi-threading concept is implemented by using
 // the following two models:
 // 1. Green Thread model
 // 2. Native OS model</pre>
<h3>04:43 — Green Thread model (board dictation)</h3>
<pre class='code'> // Green Thread model:
 // The thread which is managed completely by JVM
 // without taking underlying OS support
 // is called Green Thread
 //
 // Very few operating systems like Sun Solaris
 // provide support for Green Thread model
 //
 // Anyway Green Thread model is deprecated
 // and not recommended to use</pre>
<h3>06:49 — Native OS model (board dictation)</h3>
<pre class='code'> // Native OS model:
 // The thread which is managed by the JVM
 // with the help of underlying OS
 // is called Native OS model
 //
 // All Windows based operating systems
 // provide support for Native OS model</pre>
<p>Usually we use Native OS model, not Green Thread model.</p>
<h3>08:45 — How to stop a thread</h3>
<p>How to start a thread? t.start().</p>
<p>How to stop a thread? t.stop() — equally simple.</p>
<p>If you call stop(): thread immediately enters dead state — killing the thread in the middle of execution (“murder”).</p>
<p>Is killing a person / thread in the middle recommended? Not recommended.</p>
<p>Hence `stop()` is a deprecated method — not recommended to use.</p>
<h3>10:19 — Why stop is dangerous (DB connection example)</h3>
<p>run() job example:</p>
<ol><li>Open DB connection</li></ol>
<ol><li>Read data</li></ol>
<ol><li>Close DB connection</li></ol>
<pre class='code'>public void run() {
    // open DB connection
    // read data
    // close DB connection
}</pre>
<p>If someone calls t.stop() while the thread is reading:</p>
<ul><li>Thread enters dead state immediately.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Who closes the DB connection? No one.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Connection resource is wasted / may not be closed.</li></ul>
<p>Killing a thread mid-execution causes resource problems → method deprecated (outdated / suppressed / harmful). Being a programmer you should not use it.</p>
<h3>12:12 — Demo without stop (child continues)</h3>
<pre class='code'>class MyThread extends Thread {
    public void run() {
        for (int i = 0; i &lt; 10; i++) {
            System.out.println(&quot;Child Thread&quot;);
        }
    }
}

class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        MyThread t = new MyThread();
        t.start();
        System.out.println(&quot;End of main thread&quot;);
        // t.stop();  // commented → child continues ~10 times
    }
}
// prints (order may interleave):
End of main thread
Child Thread
Child Thread
... (happily continues ~10 times)</pre>
<p>Main did not kill/stop child → child completes.</p>
<p>Cinema joke: without hero on earth, how can heroine stay — villain kills heroine in last minute (movie reference).</p>
<h3>14:01 — Same program WITH `t.stop()` after start</h3>
<p>Main completes then calls t.stop() to kill child — “why should child execute without main?”</p>
<p>Compile shows deprecation:</p>
<p>Note: Test.java uses or overrides a deprecated API.
Note: Recompile with -Xlint:deprecation for details.</p>
<p>With -Xlint:deprecation:</p>
<p>warning: [deprecation] stop() in java.lang.Thread has been deprecated</p>
<p>t.start();
System.out.println(&quot;End of main thread&quot;);
t.stop(); // deprecated — kills child → dead state</p>
<pre class='code'>// Child does NOT print 10 times — main stopped/killed child
End of main thread
// maybe few Child Thread lines or none depending on timing</pre>
<p>Calling stop() → thread enters dead state. Recommended? No — already deprecated.</p>
<h3>15:37 — Board summary for stop()</h3>
<pre class='code'> // We can stop a thread execution by using stop() method of Thread class
public void stop();

 // If we call stop() method then immediately
 // the thread will enter into dead state
 //
 // Anyway stop() method is deprecated
 // and not recommended to use</pre>
<h3>17:25 — How to suspend and resume a thread</h3>
<p>Government-office analogy:</p>
<ul><li>Employee involved in illegal activity → first suspend services (temporary pause).</li></ul>
<ul><li>Committee analyzes.</li></ul>
<ul><li>If guilty → terminate / jail.</li></ul>
<ul><li>If not guilty → resume services (continue again).</li></ul>
<p>API:</p>
<ul><li>A thread can suspend another thread: suspend()</li></ul>
<ul><li>Resume a suspended thread: resume()</li></ul>
<pre class='code'>public void suspend();
public void resume();</pre>
<p>t.suspend() → thread enters suspended state.</p>
<p>t.resume() → suspended thread can continue execution.</p>
<p>Main may suspend child then later resume child.</p>
<p>This style is not recommended → methods are deprecated. Rare even in interview; do not use in coding.</p>
<h3>20:24 — Board: suspend / resume</h3>
<pre class='code'> // We can suspend a thread by using suspend() method of Thread class
public void suspend();
 // Immediately the thread will enter into suspended state

 // We can resume a suspended thread by using resume() method of Thread class
public void resume();
 // Then suspended thread can continue its execution

 // Anyway these methods are deprecated
 // and not recommended to use</pre>
<h3>23:22 — Full thread life-cycle diagram (interview diagram)</h3>
<p>He draws / revises the complete life cycle — says if you draw this, interviewers often won’t ask further multi-threading questions because everything appears on the diagram.</p>
<p>Basic path:</p>
<ol><li>MyThread t = new MyThread(); → New / Born state</li></ol>
<ol><li>t.start() → Ready / Runnable</li></ol>
<ol><li>Thread scheduler allocates processor → Running</li></ol>
<ol><li>run() completes → Dead</li></ol>
<h3>25:09 — Running → yield → Ready/Runnable</h3>
<p>If running thread calls Thread.yield() → goes from Running to Ready/Runnable (to give chance to waiting threads of same/similar priority).</p>
<h3>25:49 — Running → join → Waiting (blocked for joining)</h3>
<p>If running thread calls join (e.g. t2.join(), t2.join(1000), t2.join(1000, 100)):</p>
<p>→ Waiting state / blocked for joining.</p>
<p>Come out of waiting when:</p>
<ol><li>t2 completes, or</li></ol>
<ol><li>time expires (timed join), or</li></ol>
<ol><li>waiting thread got interrupted</li></ol>
<p>Then → Ready/Runnable (then Running when processor allocated).</p>
<h3>28:00 — Running → sleep → Sleeping</h3>
<p>If running thread calls Thread.sleep(1000) or sleep(1000, 100):</p>
<p>→ Sleeping state.</p>
<p>Come out when:</p>
<ol><li>time expires, or</li></ol>
<ol><li>sleeping thread got interrupted</li></ol>
<p>Then → Ready/Runnable.</p>
<h3>29:34 — Running → wait → Waiting (for notification / lock)</h3>
<p>Inter-thread communication: wait / notify / notifyAll.</p>
<p>If running thread calls obj.wait(), obj.wait(1000), or obj.wait(1000, 100):</p>
<p>→ Waiting state (waiting for notification).</p>
<p>Then typically moves to another waiting state: waiting to get the lock.</p>
<p>Come out of wait-for-notification when:</p>
<ol><li>waiting thread got notification, or</li></ol>
<ol><li>time expires, or</li></ol>
<ol><li>waiting thread got interrupted</li></ol>
<p>Then wait to get lock; when waiting thread got the lock → Ready/Runnable.</p>
<h3>31:50 — Running → suspend → Suspended; resume → Ready</h3>
<p>t.suspend() on running thread → Suspended state.</p>
<p>t.resume() → Ready/Runnable (continue services).</p>
<h3>32:32 — Running → stop → Dead</h3>
<p>t.stop() on running thread → immediately Dead state.</p>
<h3>32:51 — Life-cycle recap (spoken full tour)</h3>
<p>He retells the whole diagram once more:</p>
<ul><li>start → Ready/Runnable → processor → Running → run completes → Dead</li></ul>
<ul><li>yield → Ready/Runnable</li></ul>
<ul><li>join → Waiting (blocked for joining); leave on complete / timeout / interrupt</li></ul>
<ul><li>sleep → Sleeping; leave on timeout / interrupt</li></ul>
<ul><li>wait → Waiting; then waiting for lock; leave on notify / timeout / interrupt then got lock → Ready</li></ul>
<ul><li>suspend → Suspended; resume → Ready</li></ul>
<ul><li>stop → Dead</li></ul>
<pre class='code'> // Interview life-cycle sketch (states he names):
 // New/Born --start()--&gt; Ready/Runnable --processor--&gt; Running --run ends--&gt; Dead
 // Running --yield()--&gt; Ready/Runnable
 // Running --join()--&gt; Waiting (blocked for joining) --&gt; Ready/Runnable
 // Running --sleep()--&gt; Sleeping --&gt; Ready/Runnable
 // Running --wait()--&gt; Waiting (notify) --&gt; Waiting (for lock) --&gt; Ready/Runnable
 // Running --suspend()--&gt; Suspended --resume()--&gt; Ready/Runnable
 // Running --stop()--&gt; Dead</pre>
<h3>34:28 — Close</h3>
<p>This is the simple life cycle of a thread. Draw this diagram in interview. End of Part-14 (remaining board writing time ~35:25).</p>
